  "title": "Oil Flash Note: Port Authority",
  "type": "commodities",
  "priority": "medium",
  "published": "2026-05-19",
  "insights": [
   "Global oil trade relies on a hub-and-spoke network where 62% of seaborne oil passes through a small set of maritime chokepoints, creating significant vulnerability to disruption.",
   "Iran may be shifting its strategy from overt military threats (mines/missiles) to a legally sophisticated approach of regulating the Strait of Hormuz via 'service fees' for safety and environmental protection.",
   "By coordinating with Oman and framing charges as non-discriminatory service fees rather than transit tolls, Iran could attempt to gain tacit international legitimacy while exerting economic leverage.",
   "UNCLOS generally prohibits transit tolls, but allows charges for specific services (pilotage, towage, etc.) provided they are non-discriminatory and linked to actual services rendered.",
   "Turkey has successfully increased transit fees for the Turkish Straits by 15% as of July 2025, demonstrating how littoral states can leverage regulatory frameworks to increase revenue."
  ],
  "sector_views": [
   {
    "sector": "Energy",
